## Can we assume that the data follows a multivariate normal distribution?
- Points close to the Q-Q reference pattern would support multivariate normality; departures indicate non-normality.
- Mardia's skewness test gives p = 0.003 < 0.05, so the multivariate normality assumption is rejected.
- Mardia's kurtosis result is consistent with normality.
- Overall, because the skewness test fails, the data cannot be assumed to be multivariate normal.
![]("../Figures/QQ-plot.png"){width=60% fig-align="center"}


## Elements that aren't normally distributed 
Anderson-Darling Tests were conducted on each element to check individual normality

H0: Normally Distributed H1: Not Normally Distributed ~ $\alpha = 0.05$ 

- Anderson-Darling	20-Ca*	2.670	<0.001	Not normal

- Anderson-Darling	15-P	  3.143	<0.001	Not normal

- Anderson-Darling	25-Mn	  2.809	<0.001	Not normal

- Anderson-Darling	28-Ni	  0.812	0.032	  Not normal

Note: 15-P more than likely not normal due to large number of missing values that were transformed earlier. 

## Correlogram
![]("../Figures/Correlogram.png"){width=55% fig-align="center"}

- Red = positive correlation, blue = negative; larger circles indicate stronger relationships
- Mn, Mg, Si, Fe, Ni, Al, Ti, K, and Zn are mutually positively correlated
- Na, Ca, P, S, and Pb are negatively or uncorrelated with that group

